Reason: The GPIO device is added to the global list `gpio_devices` (via `gpiodev_add_to_list_unlocked`) before its SRCU structures are initialized. A concurrent reader in `gpio_name_to_desc()` can find the device on the list and attempt to use `&gdev->srcu` (e.g., in `__srcu_read_lock`) while it is still uninitialized, causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = init_srcu_struct(&gdev->srcu);
if (ret)
    goto err_free_label;

ret = init_srcu_struct(&gdev->desc_srcu);
if (ret)
    goto err_cleanup_gdev_srcu;

scoped_guard(mutex, &gpio_devices_lock) {
    ...
    ret = gpiodev_add_to_list_unlocked(gdev);
    if (ret) {
        gpiochip_err(gc, "GPIO integer space overlap, cannot add chip\n");
        goto err_cleanup_desc_srcu;
    }
}
```
Reason: SRCU structures are now initialized *before* the device is added to the global list (`gpiodev_add_to_list_unlocked`). This ensures that any concurrent reader that finds the device on the list will see fully initialized SRCU. The error handling labels are reordered accordingly: cleanup of `desc_srcu` and `srcu` now occurs after removal from the list (if needed), matching the reversed initialization order.

